  <p>
    <a href="/people/amin-samkough/">&larr; Amin Samkough</a> &middot;
    <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/">Research</a>
  </p>
  <hr />

  <section id="details">
    <h2>About this page</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>Article</dt>
      <dd>
        <span lang="ru">«Черкесы на Ближнем Востоке в османскую эпоху»</span>
        (“Circassians in the Middle East in the Ottoman Era”)
      </dd>
      <dt>Author</dt>
      <dd>Samir Khotko (<span lang="ru">Самир Хотко</span>)</dd>
      <dt>Published</dt>
      <dd>
        <time datetime="2013-08-05T14:00">5 August 2013, 14:00</time> on the
        website Kavkazoved (<span lang="ru">«Кавказ: новости, история,
        традиции»</span>, “Caucasus: news, history, traditions”), in its
        Publications section
      </dd>
      <dt>Source</dt>
      <dd>
        <a href={sourceUrl}>{sourceUrl}</a>
      </dd>
      <dt>Language</dt>
      <dd>Russian</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      <mark>Review: the time zone of “14:00” isn’t stated.</mark> The page also
      doesn’t say whether this was the first publication.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="same-text">
    <h2>This is the same article as one already on this site</h2>
    <p>
      I compared this page with the one at Intercircass, which is summarized on
      <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/khotko-circassians-in-syria/">
        another page of this site
      </a>. Apart from the headline and the author’s byline, the text matches
      paragraph for paragraph, including the paragraphs about Amin Samkough. For
      that reason I have not repeated the full summary here.
    </p>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the title differs.</strong> This version is called
          “Circassians in the Middle East in the Ottoman Era”. The Intercircass
          version is called “Circassians in Syria in the Ottoman Era and in the
          20th Century”. The body of both covers the 20th century and Syria in
          detail, so the title here understates what the article contains.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the order of publication.</strong> Intercircass dates
          its copy 29 December 2012, which is earlier than this one. So this
          version is not the earliest known appearance. Both may come from an
          earlier print publication, since the Intercircass copy ends with a
          cut-off reference to the newspaper “Adyge Psale”.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>two web copies are not two sources.</strong> Please
          don’t count this as a second confirmation of anything the article says.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="samkough">
    <h2>What it says about Amin Samkough</h2>
    <p>
      As in the other copy, he appears in the section on the French mandate in
      Syria. The key sentence and its translation:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p lang="ru">
        Группа активистов во главе с Амином Самгугом в 1927 г. основала в
        Кунейтре «Черкесское общество образования и единения»…
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>
          “A group of activists headed by Amin Samgug founded the ‘Circassian
          Society for Education and Unity’ in Quneitra in 1927…”
        </em>
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The article then says the society produced pamphlets and school textbooks
      in Circassian in a Latin-based alphabet, and published a newspaper called
      “Mardj” in Circassian, Arabic, Turkish and French. The next paragraph
      says a ten-year Circassian school opened in Quneitra in 1932. Nothing else
      in the article is directly about him.
    </p>
    <h3>Things to review</h3>
    <p>
      The same points apply as on the
      <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/khotko-circassians-in-syria/">
        other Khotko page
      </a>:
    </p>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: the school is dated <strong>1932</strong>, but the Mirzoev
          article and the biography page say 1933.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: the society’s name is the literal translation of its
          Circassian name. Mirzoev gives it as “Society for the Promotion of
          Education and Culture”.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: the newspaper is said to be in four languages, but the front
          page of issue 71 on the
          <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/marj-newspaper/">Marj page</a> is in
          Ottoman Turkish and Arabic only.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: the article dates Ahmad Nami’s appointment as president of the
          Syrian state to 1925. Other sources give 28 April 1926.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="translation-notes">
    <h2>Translation notes</h2>
    <p>
      I translated the quoted sentence myself from the Russian, and it should be
      checked by a fluent reader. I have not translated the article in full,
      because it is someone else’s published work. Read it in Russian at
      <a href={sourceUrl}>kavkazoved.info</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</PageLayout>

